WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.266 --> 00:00:12.687
it is common to see people on the street, riding the subway, or in the gym listening to

2
00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:15.468
music on their MP3 players through headphones.

3
00:00:16.368 --> 00:00:21.030
But most of us don't realize that personal stereo systems are a modern invention.

4
00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:27.471
In fact, the recording industry, which records music and sells it to the public, is not very old

5
00:00:27.551 --> 00:00:27.911
at all.

6
00:00:28.912 --> 00:00:35.029
Live Music For thousands of years, music has been an important part of every culture on the planet.

7
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:41.191
Before the Middle Ages, however, the only kind of music that was available had to be played live.

8
00:00:42.071 --> 00:00:47.333
Music was not recorded or written down, so the knowledge of how to play instruments and certain songs

9
00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:50.274
was passed down orally from teacher to student.

10
00:00:51.914 --> 00:00:57.256
Then, sometime before the fifteenth century, people began to use a written language to record the notes of

11
00:00:57.316 --> 00:01:00.690
songs so that they could remember them and share them with others.

12
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.593
This language is called musical notation, and it is still used today.

13
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:11.396
The advent of musical notation meant that musicians in France, for example, could learn how to play a

14
00:01:11.436 --> 00:01:15.259
piece of music written by musicians in England without ever having met them.

15
00:01:16.279 --> 00:01:22.843
The Phonograph The next major advance in music technology occurred in the late nineteenth century, when Thomas Edison

16
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:23.964
invented the phonograph.

17
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.121
This device allowed people to record sounds for the first time.

18
00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:35.603
It had two major components: a large metal horn, which focused sound waves onto a vibrating needle, and

19
00:01:35.623 --> 00:01:37.904
a rotating cylinder covered in wax.

20
00:01:38.804 --> 00:01:41.785
As the needle vibrated, it would make grooves in the wax.

21
00:01:42.745 --> 00:01:46.066
The process could be reversed to replay the sound for an audience.

22
00:01:47.286 --> 00:01:52.948
People could now play their favorite songs whenever they wanted, so they didn't have to rely on live

23
00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:53.908
musicians anymore.

24
00:01:55.048 --> 00:02:00.571
In the beginning of the twentieth century, the cylinder was replaced with a spinning disk called a "78."

25
00:02:00.811 --> 00:02:07.674
This phonograph changed the type of music that people listened to because a 78 could record only three

26
00:02:07.714 --> 00:02:08.754
minutes on each side.

27
00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:12.616
So, many songs were shortened to fit the format of the medium.

28
00:02:13.597 --> 00:02:16.438
Also, the quality of the phonograph recording was quite low.

29
00:02:17.218 --> 00:02:20.900
Musicians had to play loudly to ensure that their music would be picked up.

30
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.624
Higher tones, such as the voices of sopranos, were easier to record than low tones.

31
00:02:27.685 --> 00:02:31.208
Among the orchestral instruments, trumpets and violins worked best.

32
00:02:31.929 --> 00:02:34.711
For folk music, banjos were better than guitars.

33
00:02:35.612 --> 00:02:41.037
As a result, the first music to be recorded, such as the tunes played by jazz bands, featured

34
00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:42.118
these kinds of instruments.

35
00:02:43.479 --> 00:02:49.104
Radio By 1926, a new invention, the radio, was spreading across the country.

36
00:02:49.888 --> 00:02:55.430
The old mechanical equipment was replaced by microphones, which are more sensitive and can pick up a wider

37
00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:56.211
range of tones.

38
00:02:57.251 --> 00:03:03.093
Because singers and musicians no longer had to play at top volume, more delicate and complex styles could

39
00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:03.674
be captured.

40
00:03:04.674 --> 00:03:11.817
Bing Crosby, who eventually made more than 1,700 recordings, was among the first singers to take advantage of

41
00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:12.617
these developments.

42
00:03:13.578 --> 00:03:18.220
Instead of singing as if he were performing in a large concert hall, he sang softly into the

43
00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.201
microphone in an intimate style.

44
00:03:21.182 --> 00:03:26.586
He and other male singers like him, including Frank Sinatra, dominated the airwaves for years.

45
00:03:27.747 --> 00:03:32.831
Their popularity soared even higher when smaller, portable radios became widely available.

46
00:03:33.872 --> 00:03:38.296
Disc jockeys, often called DJs, would ask their listeners about their favorite tunes.

47
00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:45.221
Over time, this process resulted in "top-ten" lists of the most requested songs of the day, which would

48
00:03:45.261 --> 00:03:49.448
become known as pop─short for "popular"─music.

49
00:03:49.468 --> 00:03:55.992
Around the same time, a recording company called RCA Victor introduced albums that were more durable and able

50
00:03:56.032 --> 00:03:57.152
to hold more information.

51
00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:01.535
These new long-playing records, called LPs, also sounded better.

52
00:04:02.255 --> 00:04:06.497
The recordings could be more subtle and complex, and up to 23 minutes long.

53
00:04:07.398 --> 00:04:12.561
These were sold by the millions and helped launch the careers of hundreds of folk, country, blues, and

54
00:04:12.621 --> 00:04:13.261
pop singers.

55
00:04:14.622 --> 00:04:21.718
Les Paul and Tape Another development in the recording industry came after the Second World War when American

56
00:04:21.778 --> 00:04:26.981
engineer Les Paul was introduced to a piece of equipment used by the German military.

57
00:04:27.942 --> 00:04:33.946
It used thin strips of paper or plastic coated with metal particles arranged in a way that represents

58
00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:41.271
sounds. Significantly, these tapes could be used to record sounds, erased, and then used to record again.

59
00:04:41.992 --> 00:04:46.095
This meant that musicians didn't have to make their performances perfect the first time.

60
00:04:47.075 --> 00:04:49.676
If they made a mistake, they could just try again.

61
00:04:50.397 --> 00:04:56.158
Tape had another benefit as well; you could record several sounds separately, and combine them into the final

62
00:04:56.218 --> 00:04:56.958
recording later.

63
00:04:57.719 --> 00:05:02.440
This clever technique, multi-tracking, dominated professional recording for decades.

64
00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:09.622
After he mastered the art of recording, Paul went on to invent another musical tool: he built the

65
00:05:09.702 --> 00:05:11.903
first solid-body electric guitar.

66
00:05:12.783 --> 00:05:16.584
This new instrument was loud and unlike anything else ever heard!

67
00:05:17.448 --> 00:05:23.772
It appealed to young people, especially during the 1950s and '60s, and contributed to a new genre of

68
00:05:23.832 --> 00:05:25.532
music called rock and roll.

69
00:05:26.633 --> 00:05:32.096
This genre was transformed─as was the music industry yet again─in the 1970s and '80s.

70
00:05:33.056 --> 00:05:36.198
After the mid-1960s, the cassette tape was developed.

71
00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:41.741
Much smaller than regular recording tape, it was placed in a plastic shell for protection.

72
00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:48.237
Because it was so compact in size and simple to use, cassette tape gave people the chance to

73
00:05:48.297 --> 00:05:50.998
record their music easily and share it with friends.

74
00:05:51.898 --> 00:05:55.820
This led to a surge in the number and variety of artists on the music scene.

75
00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:04.123
The Synthesizer and Digital Technology Most people were satisfied with both LPs and cassette tapes.

76
00:06:04.223 --> 00:06:10.946
In 1960, however, an enterprising engineer, Robert Moog, built a machine that could make sounds out of electrical

77
00:06:11.006 --> 00:06:13.007
signals, called a synthesizer.

78
00:06:14.007 --> 00:06:19.245
At first, it was slow to catch on, but in the 1980s, people began to buy these machines

79
00:06:19.305 --> 00:06:20.006
for their homes.

80
00:06:20.826 --> 00:06:26.088
Because the synthesizer was easily hooked up to a computer and simple to play, it took off.

81
00:06:26.908 --> 00:06:33.991
This new digital technology allowed people to manipulate rhythms and beats, expanding several musical genres such as techno

82
00:06:34.191 --> 00:06:34.732
and hip hop.

83
00:06:35.672 --> 00:06:42.054
Meanwhile, recording companies were desperate to find a medium that could last longer and capture higher-quality recordings.

84
00:06:42.935 --> 00:06:49.458
Finally, in 1982, Sony and Phillips released a new kind of record, the compact disc (CD).

85
00:06:50.338 --> 00:06:55.600
Shaped like a smaller LP, the CD could hold over an hour of music, and it sounded crystal

86
00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:02.924
clear. The CD remained the most popular music medium for over two decades, but recently, the Internet has

87
00:07:02.984 --> 00:07:05.125
begun to have a profound effect on the industry.

88
00:07:05.905 --> 00:07:11.588
Since 1991, computer users have been able to compress sound into small files called MP3s.

89
00:07:13.024 --> 00:07:18.428
These can be stored on a portable storage device, such as an iPod, or sent to other users

90
00:07:18.588 --> 00:07:19.349
over the Internet.

91
00:07:20.129 --> 00:07:25.293
Because the files are digital data, they can be erased, modified, and copied easily.

92
00:07:26.154 --> 00:07:32.519
More importantly, MP3s can be uploaded and downloaded from the Internet, so music lovers can shop for a

93
00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:37.362
wide variety of songs from anywhere in the world as long as they have an Internet connection.

94
00:07:38.303 --> 00:07:43.865
With this level of convenience, it's no wonder that MP3s are quickly replacing the CD in popularity.

95
00:07:44.786 --> 00:07:47.487
The recording industry will undoubtedly continue to change.

96
00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:54.169
The standards of consumers keep on rising and the quality of recordings must continue to meet those expectations.

97
00:07:55.129 --> 00:07:58.170
Who knows what heights recorded sound will reach in the next century?
